Booking a parcel takes four calls across three APIs. The diagram below is generated from
[`ship-a-parcel.arazzo.yaml`](/workflows/ship-a-parcel.arazzo.yaml), an
[Arazzo](https://spec.openapis.org/arazzo/latest.html) description of the journey: each box is one
operation, the dashed lines are the values one step passes to the next, and the outputs at the
bottom are what you have at the end.

{% workflow file="ship-a-parcel" id="shipParcel" title="Ship a parcel" /%}

## Running it

**Run workflow** executes the steps from your browser against the server in each spec (the
mock server, by default). The Shipments steps need a token with `shipments:write` and
`shipments:read`; paste one in the credentials fields of the run panel, or sign in on the
[Shipments API](/apis/shipments) page and use the same token. The Rates and Tracking steps run
without one.

- **quote** prices the parcel (Rates, public).
- **book** creates the shipment for that quote. A `409` means the `reference` was used before;
  the workflow stops, since the shipment already exists.
- **label** fetches the PDF label.
- **subscribe** registers your callback URL for the parcel's `HUB`, `OFD` and `DLV` events.

A `503` from any step is retried up to three times, two seconds apart.

## The same workflow for AI agents

The portal's MCP export turns this workflow into one tool, `ship_parcel`, whose arguments are the
workflow's inputs (`reference`, `service`, `callbackUrl`) and whose result is its outputs. An
agent that can call the tool gets the four steps, the retries and the checks for free.
